    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which parts of an exterior window should be caulked?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Seal the joints that face weather: where side casings meet siding, the top corners and miters of the trim, the joint between the window frame and exterior trim on the top and sides, and where siding meets the head trim unless a drip cap handles it. Leave the sill underside, weep slots and other drainage paths at the bottom open."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What type of caulk is best around outside windows?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For painted wood trim, an elastomeric latex sealant such as DAP Dynaflex 230 is easy to apply and paintable. For vinyl windows, vinyl siding or fiber cement, a polymer sealant such as OSI Quad bonds better. For glass and unpainted metal, use 100 percent silicone. Avoid plain painter's caulk, which cracks quickly on exterior trim."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Must the old caulk come out before recaulking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, wherever it has cracked, pulled away or hardened. New sealant laid over old caulk bonds only to the failing material, so both fail together. Cut along both edges, pull and scrape the old bead, and clean the joint. Old silicone leaves a film that blocks adhesion, so scrub it with a residue remover until the surface is clean."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the lowest temperature for caulking windows?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
