            "text": "Most latex sealants list a minimum around 40 degrees Fahrenheit for both the air and the surface, and they need to stay above freezing until they skin. Some polymer and solvent-based sealants are labeled for colder application. Measure the surface temperature on shaded sides, and finish early enough that the bead skins before evening dew forms."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does window caulk last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Plain acrylic caulk may fail in a few years on sunny exposures. A quality elastomeric latex or polymer sealant laid on a clean, properly sized joint and protected by paint commonly lasts many years, and many products carry long durability claims on the label. Inspect each spring and fall and patch failures early to avoid larger repairs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I use backer rod around windows?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use it on any joint deeper than about half its width, which is common around older wood windows and brick openings. Choose closed-cell foam rod about 25 percent wider than the gap and seat it so the sealant ends up about half as deep as it is wide. Shallow joints that cannot take rod can use bond-breaker tape instead."
          }
