    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A good window caulk job takes about 30 minutes per window and one tube of the right sealant. A bad one takes the same time and can trap water inside the wall for years. The difference comes down to three decisions: which joints you seal, which joints you deliberately leave open, and whether you remove the old bead before laying the new one.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="how-water-gets-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Water Actually Gets Past a Window
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before picking up a caulk gun, it helps to understand what exterior caulk around a window is supposed to do. On a properly installed window, caulk is the outermost line of defense, not the only one. Behind it sit the window's nailing flange, flashing tape lapped over that flange, and the weather-resistive barrier on the wall, all overlapped so water runs down and out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caulk's job is to keep the bulk of wind-driven rain from reaching those hidden layers, and to close gaps where trim meets siding so water does not run behind the casing. When the caulk fails, the hidden layers take the load. On a well-flashed window that may be fine for a while. On an older house with little or no flashing, a failed bead is often the only thing between rain and the wall framing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water usually enters at a few predictable places: the top corners where head trim meets side casing, the joint above the head trim where siding meets it, the joint between the side casings and siding, and around the sill where the sill horn meets siding. The top of the window matters most because water arrives there first and gravity drives it into any gap below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Signs that a window needs attention include peeling paint on the trim, staining or swelling at the bottom of the casing, soft wood at the sill, or water stains on the interior drywall below the window. If you find soft wood, fix it before you seal; caulk over rot just hides it. Dig out the damaged fibers and rebuild with an epoxy product from our <a href="/best-wood-filler/">wood filler guide</a>, prime, and then caulk.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PRINCIPLE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Seal the top and sides. Let the bottom drain.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Water that gets behind trim has to have a way out. Close the joints that face the weather, and leave the drainage paths at the bottom of the window open so anything that does get in can escape.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="seal-or-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Joints to Seal, and Gaps to Leave Open
          </h2>

          <p>
            Window caulking goes wrong most often through enthusiasm: sealing every visible crack, including the ones that exist to let water out. Walk around the window and sort every joint into one of two lists before you start.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Joint</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Seal it?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Head trim to siding (above window)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, unless a drip cap is present</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stops water running behind trim</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Side casing to siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Faces wind-driven rain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Trim miters and corners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common entry point at the top</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Frame to trim (exterior stop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, sides and top</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Closes the gap around the unit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sill underside and drip edge</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lets trapped water drain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weep slots in vinyl or aluminum frames</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Built-in drainage</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Metal drip cap over head trim</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually no</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Designed to shed water outward</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The head trim deserves a closer look. Many houses have a metal or vinyl drip cap that tucks up behind the siding and laps over the top of the head trim. That cap is designed to throw water clear. Caulking along its top edge can trap water behind it; caulking its front edge closed blocks its drainage. Seal only where the siding meets the cap if the manufacturer of the siding calls for it, and leave the cap itself alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinyl and aluminum windows usually have small weep slots in the bottom of the frame. They let rain that gets past the sash drain to the outside. Painting or caulking them shut is one of the most common causes of water pooling in the sill track and leaking inside. Check that they are open and clear them with a thin wire if needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The underside of a wood sill and the bottom edge of a window's nailing flange in many modern installations are also drainage points. Building science guidance and most window installation instructions call for the bottom flange to be left unsealed, or sealed only with gaps, so water that reaches the flashing can exit. When in doubt about the bottom of a window, leave it open.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Tools, Materials and the Right Sealant
          </h2>

          <p>
            The kit is short and inexpensive. You need a utility knife or a hooked caulk removal tool, a stiff putty knife or five-in-one tool, a wire or nylon brush, rags, isopropyl alcohol, painter's tape for visible joints, closed-cell backer rod for deep gaps, a smooth dripless caulk gun, and a plastic tooling spoon or a bucket of water for wet-finger tooling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sealant choice depends on what the window and trim are made of and whether you will paint. For painted wood trim, an elastomeric latex sealant such as DAP Dynaflex 230 or Sashco Big Stretch is easy to work, paintable and flexible enough for seasonal movement. For vinyl windows, vinyl siding or fiber cement, a paintable polymer sealant such as OSI Quad grips better. For glass, aluminum or unpainted metal, use 100 percent silicone. The full comparison is in our <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Planning Numbers for One Window</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">18-20 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Perimeter of a typical double-hung</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1 tube</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">10.1 oz at a 1/4-inch bead</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30-45 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hands-on time per window</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">40°F+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum for most latex sealants</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Avoid plain painter's caulk for exterior window joints. It is cheap and easy to tool, but most products of that type have very limited movement capacity and crack within a season or two on trim that heats and cools in the sun. Look for a product with an ASTM C920 movement class on the label, or at minimum one sold explicitly as an elastomeric exterior sealant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the weather too. You want a dry spell with surface temperatures comfortably above the label minimum, no rain for at least the label's rain-ready time, and ideally a mild temperature so the joint is at a mid-range width. If the forecast is uncertain, our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-caulk-take-to-dry/">how long caulk takes to dry</a> lists typical rain-ready windows for each sealant type.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Remove the Old Bead and Prepare the Joint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Preparation is where most of the time goes, and it is what decides how long the new bead lasts. New sealant laid over old caulk bonds only to that old caulk, so when the old bead lets go, both come off together. Plan to remove every failing bead completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Score along both edges of the old bead with a sharp blade held at a shallow angle, then hook the bead out with a removal tool or pull it with pliers. Hardened latex often comes out in pieces; scrape the remainder with a stiff putty knife. Be careful not to gouge vinyl or soft wood. Old silicone leaves an invisible film, so scrub the joint with a silicone residue remover or mineral spirits and a nylon brush until water no longer beads on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, clean. Brush out dirt, spider webs and paint flakes. Treat any mildew with a diluted bleach solution, rinse and let it dry. Wipe both sides of the joint with a rag dampened with isopropyl alcohol to remove dust and oil. Then let the joint dry thoroughly; latex sealants tolerate slight dampness poorly, and most products of all types bond best to clean, dry surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect the wood now that the joint is open. Probe the sill and the bottom of the casings with an awl. If the point sinks in easily, the wood is soft. Small soft spots can be consolidated and filled with a two-part epoxy repair product; larger areas mean replacing the board. Prime any bare wood before caulking, since most sealants bond better to primed wood than to raw, thirsty end grain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, size the gap. For joints deeper than about half their width, push in closed-cell backer rod roughly 25 percent wider than the gap, so that the sealant depth will be about half the width. Use a blunt tool to seat it evenly and avoid puncturing it. For shallow joints where rod will not fit, a strip of bond-breaker tape at the bottom serves the same purpose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Laying and Tooling the Bead
          </h2>

          <p>
            On visible joints, run painter's tape along both sides of the joint, leaving the gap exposed. This gives a crisp line and makes cleanup trivial. Skip it on rough or hidden joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle at about a 45-degree angle, starting with a small opening and enlarging it if needed. The opening should be a little narrower than the joint so sealant is forced in rather than laid on top. Puncture the inner seal with the gun's wire, and if you are using a fresh tube after a break, run a short test bead on cardboard to clear any hardened plug.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start at the top of the window and work down so you are not dragging the gun through fresh sealant. Hold the gun at about 45 degrees and push the bead ahead of the nozzle, rather than pulling it behind. Pushing packs sealant into the joint and against both sides. Keep a steady trigger pressure and move at a speed that fills the joint fully with a slight crown. Release pressure at the end of each run; a dripless gun stops the flow automatically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tool each run immediately, before it skins. Press a tooling spoon or a wet finger along the bead in one smooth pass, forcing the sealant into contact with both sides and leaving a slightly concave surface. Work in runs no longer than you can tool within a few minutes, especially with fast-skinning silicone and polymer products. Wipe excess off the tool frequently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pull the tape while the sealant is still wet, pulling away from the bead at a low angle. If you wait until it skins, the tape tears the bead edge. Clean any smears immediately with water for latex, or the solvent the label specifies for silicone and polymers. Cured polymer on vinyl siding is very hard to remove without leaving a mark.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corners and miters</h3>

          <p>
            At the top corners, where head trim meets side casing, fill the miter first and tool it into the joint, then run the horizontal and vertical beads into it. Overlapping beads at the corner ensures there is no seam for water to follow. These top corners are the single most important spots on the window; take an extra minute on each.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="special-cases" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Vinyl Windows, Brick Openings and Stucco
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl windows move more than wood with temperature, and vinyl's smooth surface is harder for latex to grip. Use a polymer sealant rated for vinyl, ideally in a color matched to the frame if it will not be painted. Do not caulk the J-channel that surrounds many vinyl windows on vinyl-sided houses; it is a drainage channel, and sealing it traps water. Seal only where the manufacturer or siding instructions call for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Windows set in brick openings usually have a wider, irregular gap between the frame or brickmold and the masonry. Polymer or tripolymer sealants handle the porous, alkaline brick and the large movement between dissimilar materials far better than latex. Wire-brush loose mortar from the edges, use backer rod generously because these gaps are often deep, and leave brick weep holes below the window open.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stucco openings are similar. Many stucco installations have a deliberate sealant joint between the stucco and the window frame, sometimes with a casing bead that defines the joint. Clean out the old sealant, rod the joint, and use a paintable polymer sealant that bonds to cementitious surfaces. Hairline cracks radiating from the window corners in stucco are a separate issue and usually need a stucco patch rather than caulk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aluminum and steel frames, common on older and commercial-style buildings, are best sealed with silicone at the glass and a paintable polymer where the frame meets the wall if the wall will be painted. On any metal, clean off oxidation and oils with alcohol before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the material, the logic stays the same: seal the joints that face the weather, keep drainage paths open, size the joint properly, and choose a sealant that bonds to both sides. If you are unsure whether a gap is a drainage path, check the window or siding manufacturer's installation instructions before closing it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Curing, Painting and Keeping the Seal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Leave the joints alone while they cure. Latex sealants usually skin in under an hour and can take light rain after a few hours, but the core stays soft for days. Polymer sealants follow their own label times. Keep sprinklers off the area for the first day and avoid washing the windows with a hose or pressure washer for at least a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint the trim once the label's paint window has passed. Use a quality exterior acrylic paint and carry it a few millimeters onto the bead and onto the siding on each side. The paint shields latex sealant from UV and seals the bond line, which is where water tries to creep in first. Do not paint silicone; paint will not adhere to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your windows twice a year, ideally in spring and fall, looking for beads that have pulled away from one side, cracked through, hardened or shrunk. Small failures can be cut out and patched; widespread failure means a full redo. A good elastomeric or polymer sealant laid on a clean, properly sized joint and protected by paint commonly lasts many years before that becomes necessary.
          </p>

          <p>
            Window caulk works best when the rest of the wall is helping. Clogged gutters that overflow above a window can dump far more water on the head trim than rain alone, which is one reason <a href="/best-gutter-guards/">gutter guards</a> are worth considering on houses with heavy tree cover. Keep the wood trim itself protected with sound paint or a finish such as those in our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the bottom of the window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The sill underside, weep slots in vinyl frames and the bottom nailing flange on many installations exist to let water out. Caulking them shut traps any water that gets behind the trim, leading to rot in the sill and framing that may not show for years. Seal the top and sides; leave the bottom drainage paths open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying new caulk over a cracked bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It looks faster, but new sealant only bonds to the old caulk, which has already failed. When the old bead lets go, the new one goes with it, often within a season. Cut out the old material, scrape and clean the joint to bare surfaces, and only then lay the fresh bead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping backer rod in deep gaps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brick and older wood openings often have gaps far deeper than they are wide. Filling them solid with sealant wastes tubes, slows curing and creates a bead that cannot stretch properly. Push in closed-cell backer rod so the sealant depth is roughly half the joint width, then caulk over it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dragging the gun instead of pushing it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling the nozzle along the joint lays sealant on top of the gap rather than forcing it in, leaving voids behind the bead and weak edges. Push the bead ahead of the nozzle at about 45 degrees so the sealant packs into the joint, then tool it immediately to press it against both sides.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using interior painter's caulk outside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Basic acrylic painter's caulk is cheap and tools beautifully, but it has very little stretch and degrades in sun. On window trim that heats and cools daily it typically cracks within a season or two. Choose an elastomeric latex or a polymer sealant rated for exterior use and seasonal movement.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which parts of an exterior window should be caulked?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seal the joints that face weather: where side casings meet siding, the top corners and miters of the trim, the joint between the window frame and exterior trim on the top and sides, and where siding meets the head trim unless a drip cap handles it. Leave the sill underside, weep slots and other drainage paths at the bottom open.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of caulk is best around outside windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For painted wood trim, an elastomeric latex sealant such as DAP Dynaflex 230 is easy to apply and paintable. For vinyl windows, vinyl siding or fiber cement, a polymer sealant such as OSI Quad bonds better. For glass and unpainted metal, use 100 percent silicone. Avoid plain painter's caulk, which cracks quickly on exterior trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Must the old caulk come out before recaulking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, wherever it has cracked, pulled away or hardened. New sealant laid over old caulk bonds only to the failing material, so both fail together. Cut along both edges, pull and scrape the old bead, and clean the joint. Old silicone leaves a film that blocks adhesion, so scrub it with a residue remover until the surface is clean.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the lowest temperature for caulking windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most latex sealants list a minimum around 40 degrees Fahrenheit for both the air and the surface, and they need to stay above freezing until they skin. Some polymer and solvent-based sealants are labeled for colder application. Measure the surface temperature on shaded sides, and finish early enough that the bead skins before evening dew forms.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does window caulk last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plain acrylic caulk may fail in a few years on sunny exposures. A quality elastomeric latex or polymer sealant laid on a clean, properly sized joint and protected by paint commonly lasts many years, and many products carry long durability claims on the label. Inspect each spring and fall and patch failures early to avoid larger repairs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use backer rod around windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use it on any joint deeper than about half its width, which is common around older wood windows and brick openings. Choose closed-cell foam rod about 25 percent wider than the gap and seat it so the sealant ends up about half as deep as it is wide. Shallow joints that cannot take rod can use bond-breaker tape instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I caulk over painted trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if the paint is sound. Sealant bonds well to firmly adhered paint. Scrape away anything loose or peeling, clean off chalky residue with a damp rag and let it dry, and wipe with alcohol before caulking. If the paint is failing broadly, scrape and prime the trim first, then caulk, then repaint over the cured bead.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

