        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Caulking exterior windows well comes down to a short list: seal the top and sides, leave the bottom drainage paths open, remove the old bead completely, size deep gaps with backer rod, and push a bead of the right sealant into a clean, dry joint, then tool it straight away.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose an elastomeric latex for painted wood, a polymer for vinyl, fiber cement and masonry, and silicone for glass and unpainted metal. Give the bead time to cure, paint it once the label allows, and check it every spring and fall. One tube and an hour per window is cheap insurance against rot inside the wall.
          </p>
        </div>

